al{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184071 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184071 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/a7/a7171d7bdd2b5c79b55ce6bd204e36304640468bd2ad533ac4032cd5a4ddc2ae.svg) \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184071 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/ab/ab1cd4be22e0d3344318108914d506f9fc259446654eefff94a36d38afac3f8b.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/e7/e7660dfd412f13c5d20dd9e77ec439cdbf2916ebfbccc383d13b5f9ed4032cda.svg) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184124 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184124 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184124 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184171 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184172 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049340 #edb/ref 17592186045417]