\\text{rank}(A)+3 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $6\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "245a8171-aeb5-5caf-950d-44cbb3c0aece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184756 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67b28820-a085-5f18-884d-33b6d40e4f35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184759 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 6$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a160f7a5-3a4d-5809-8834-75195b7301e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184760 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1061 19992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af600efe-5480-5b61-8280-594a0825dbc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1061 19993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "526a000d-4f1e-513e-83a7-dcec4069077d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1061 19994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10431142-f052-51d9-b9c7-38e48410c92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1061 37511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c8115af-900b-592a-a3df-6c126d87446c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1064 "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n![](images/30/30f6472f02a4ac47f285fff05e0cdbf56367acdd18231271dc3dccf0040901bd.svg)\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n![](images/b5/b5f38590a71a2f8e1f29a5ba98920a10037737c1ebcbd3fd60f08a3e47555141.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1065 3524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0728c510-110b-5bde-93ea-9050a87a6ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [