#edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319699 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64bf6646-5170-5bb1-8e05-e24752360acc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319770 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319770 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319770 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e96af98-5bfb-5e34-afca-b9cf9d10ef39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319961 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319961 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc3cbb5a-6d77-51f2-bfe2-db9526ea77ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320125 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320125 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320125 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f454b58e-29cd-5edd-8ccd-449ab4ecf6fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186320125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "81bf6810-4bfe-5e20-bf04-b7a714fed99f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fad5527-7b65-5529-b82c-0602148a162d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321066 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452718 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452719 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452720 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "322fb855-3d8d-59e6-ba9e-0f5421e84a86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348221 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452721 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452722 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452723 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "251cb9e2-c851-5082-b166-e39206cbabf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452724 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452725 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452726 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n- **II.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92ccb45d-8792-5a6a-9a10-b7498ac220cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348223 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0131f756-450c-5ffd-bd43-d0c24b54342a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0ef6c019-6203-53f2-a536-cc2b8d76a9fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aca86402-c665-55ad-96e2-e7faee870cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "701ec281-64bf-55a9-a13a-9af209eaff5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377760 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93249bda-5da5-5cfd-8d15-56c91976394d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377761 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377761 #edb/r